<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  fix/loop-behaviour, issue h. AN AUDIBLE GAP AT A CYCLE WRAP, end to end
  through the REAL playback path (readahead + the capture backend), not just
  the engine-level harness `playback_test.cc` gates the mechanism with
  (see its new "Cycle wrap ACROSS MULTIPLE PAGES" case, which measures the
  same defect in isolation and is what should be read first for the
  arithmetic).

  Env, set on the CTest entry: SMARAGD_CAPTURE_SPEED=1; RUN_SERIAL (a
  wall-clock/continuity assertion — CLAUDE.md's "When to run it SERIALLY").

  THE SHAPE THAT BREAKS. The cycle region here is 150000 frames — a little
  over TWO 65536-frame pages — deliberately more than one page: the readahead
  window used to be purely linear in the playhead, so approaching the loop
  end its whole ~3s window was spent on pages PAST it that could never play,
  and no page at the loop start was ever demanded ahead of time. A loop that
  fits inside a single page never shows this (updateFrozenPage finds the
  SAME page it already holds), which is why the pre-existing single-page
  regression test in playback_test.cc never caught it.

  PLAYBACK IS SEEDED NEAR THE LOOP END (locator 130000 of a 0..150000
  region), not at loopStart_ (0) — mirroring record_loop_late_start.qxa's
  own reason for not starting at the loop start (main/shell/CONTRACT.md
  inv. 27). Starting AT loopStart_ makes ordinary forward playback freeze
  its pages "for free" as a side effect of minBufferFrames_'s own ~3s
  lookahead (144000 frames, close to this loop's own 150000), which would
  keep them warm regardless of whether the fix's own pre-fetch (H1) ever
  ran — measured: with only the sapplication.cpp cycle-sync fix below and
  NONE of the readahead changes, that seed shows the SAME small gap this
  case's threshold allows, proving nothing. Seeding at 130000 instead means
  loopStart_'s pages are reached by NOTHING except the mechanism under test,
  and the FIRST wrap arrives in well under half a second — before an
  unfixed linear readahead could ever have looked at position 0 by
  accident.

  test_sawtooth.wav is 192000 frames (4 s) of continuous, non-silent
  material — comfortably longer than the 150000-frame loop, so nothing in
  the loop region is ever silent BY CONTENT; any silence assert-audio-
  continuity finds is the defect, not the fixture.

  THE WAIT IS WALL-CLOCK, not a playhead target: the playhead never exceeds
  the loop end while cycling, so there is no absolute position past it to
  wait for. 10 s from a locator 20000 frames short of the wrap crosses it
  almost immediately and then several full 150000-frame (3.125 s) passes
  after — several complete wraps inside the recording, with margin for
  scheduling jitter.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="playback_loop_wrap_continuity">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- The cycle region: a little over two pages. set-property takes a
         JSON ARRAY. -->
    <set-property key="rangeStart" value="[0]"/>
    <set-property key="rangeEnd" value="[150000]"/>
    <set-property key="rangeValid" value="[true]"/>
    <cycle-enable/>

    <!-- 20000 frames short of the wrap — see the header for why this,
         and not 0, is what makes the case mean anything. -->
    <set-locator position="130000"/>
    <toggle-playback play="1"/>
    <wait-ms ms="10000"/>
    <toggle-playback play="0"/>

    <dump-playback-capture filename="playback_loop_wrap_continuity.wav"
                           minFrames="400000"/>

    <!-- No run of frames below silenceLevel may be longer than 8192 frames
         (170 ms): measured post-fix, the FIRST wrap (the readahead's
         earliest chance to pre-fetch the loop-start pages, which is why it
         is the tightest one) costs 4908 frames — the scheduler demand for
         the loop-start pages has to complete once before they are warm, and
         the first pass is the only one that cannot have started it early.
         Every wrap after the first is gap-free (the pre-fetch is already
         warm from the pass before). 8192 is a margin over 4908, not the
         house live-lane bound (CLAUDE.md's 1024 frames) restated exactly —
         that bound is for a DIFFERENT mechanism (a live lane's ring), and
         this fix's own first-wrap cost is real and larger. The first 4096
         frames are skipped: this is the CAPTURE backend's own paced start,
         not the mechanism under test. Pre-fix (see the header) this same
         measurement is two orders of magnitude larger. -->
    <assert-audio-continuity filename="playback_loop_wrap_continuity.wav"
                             channel="0" startFrame="4096" frameCount="-1"
                             maxGapFrames="8192" silenceLevel="0.02"/>

    <assert-render-policy liveThreadRefusals="0" liveOwnedRefusals="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
